smoltcp: a SYN reopens a connection in TIME-WAIT

A SYN to a connection in TIME-WAIT was dropped without an answer ("every
packet after the initial SYN must be an acknowledgement") for the 10 s
of the TIME-WAIT. The peer has the port again: Linux hands a loopback
port out again within seconds, and the host of the boot tests did, for
the connection the listen test had just closed; QEMU then sent the SYN
again only on its timer, 6 s and 18 s later, and the smoke was one
connection short (issue #656). RFC 1122 4.2.2.13 lets a SYN from after
what the peer sent on the old connection (so no old duplicate) open a
new connection from TIME-WAIT, with sequence numbers above the old
one's, and Linux does so. Now the interface ends the TIME-WAIT for such
a SYN and gives it to a listener, whose new connection starts 65537
above the old one's last sequence number, as Linux's does. With a test
(tests/ipv4.rs). Derived from smoltcp, so under smoltcp's license
(0BSD).

